---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import RangeHoodExtractionModeler from '../../components/RangeHoodExtractionModeler.astro';

const pageTitle = "Kitchen Exhaust Range Hood VOC, NO2 & PM2.5 Extraction Efficiency Modeler | IsItKitchenSafe";
const pageDescription = "Model gas stove nitrogen dioxide plumes, cooking oil PM2.5 aerosol dispersal, duct aerodynamic friction, capture velocity, and pediatric asthma inhalation risks.";

const breadcrumbs = [
  { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { label: 'Tools', href: '/tools/' },
  { label: 'Range Hood Extraction Efficiency Modeler' }
];

const faqs = [
  {
    question: "Do ductless recirculating range hoods remove nitrogen dioxide (NO2) from gas stoves?",
    answer: "No, virtually 0%. Ductless recirculating hoods draw air through an aluminum grease mesh and an activated charcoal pad, then blow the air right back into the kitchen ceiling. While activated carbon can adsorb heavy volatile organic compounds and cooking odors, it possesses zero chemical affinity or catalytic capacity for small inorganic gas molecules like Nitrogen Dioxide (NO2) and Carbon Monoxide (CO). When you operate a gas range under a ductless hood, 100% of the toxic combustion byproducts remain inside your living space."
  },
  {
    question: "How much CFM (Cubic Feet per Minute) do I actually need for a gas range?",
    answer: "The Home Ventilating Institute (HVI) and ASHRAE mandate a minimum guideline of 100 CFM of delivered airflow for every 10,000 BTU of total gas cooktop output. For instance, a standard 30-inch 4-burner residential range producing 40,000 BTU requires at least 400 CFM. A high-output professional 6-burner range (75,000 to 90,000 BTU) requires 750 to 1,000+ CFM. For electric or induction cooktops, standard sizing requires 10 to 12 CFM per linear inch of cooktop width (e.g., 360 CFM for a 30-inch cooktop)."
  },
  {
    question: "Why does cooking on the back burners capture smoke so much better than the front?",
    answer: "This is governed by the 'backsplash air-guide effect'. The vertical rear wall or tile backsplash acts as a physical boundary plate that prevents air entrainment behind the rising thermal plume, directing the hot buoyant air straight upward into the hood sump. Conversely, front burners are exposed to 360-degree ambient room drafts. Furthermore, most residential range hoods are only 18 to 20 inches deep, while cooktops are 24 inches deep, leaving front pans completely uncovered by the hood's physical capture perimeter."
  },
  {
    question: "Does gas stove cooking really cause childhood asthma?",
    answer: "Yes, extensive epidemiological and clinical evidence supports this link. A landmark 2023 meta-analysis published in the International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health (Gruenwald et al.) calculated that 12.7% of current childhood asthma cases across the United States are directly attributable to gas stove usage. In poorly ventilated homes, peak indoor nitrogen dioxide (NO2) levels regularly hit 150 to 400 ppb—far exceeding the US EPA outdoor 1-hour standard of 100 ppb and the WHO safe threshold of 106 ppb (200 µg/m³)."
  },
  {
    question: "Why should you never install flexible ribbed aluminum ducting for a kitchen hood?",
    answer: "Flexible corrugated accordion ducting creates severe internal aerodynamic turbulence. The repeating internal ridges disrupt smooth laminar airflow, increasing static backpressure and reducing actual blower flow rate by up to 50%. More dangerously, aerosolized cooking grease condenses and pools in the accordion ridges, transforming the ductwork into a combustible grease trap that poses a severe residential chimney fire hazard. Building codes and professional HVAC standards strictly recommend smooth, rigid galvanized steel ducting."
  },
  {
    question: "What is make-up air, and when do I need a make-up air damper system?",
    answer: "A range hood is an exhaust fan that removes air from your home. In tightly sealed, energy-efficient modern houses, running a high-powered exhaust hood (typically >400 CFM according to IRC building codes) creates negative air pressure (building depressurization). Without dedicated 'make-up air' introduced through an automated damper, the hood can back-draft deadly carbon monoxide from gas water heaters, furnaces, or fireplaces down their flues into living spaces. If you lack a make-up air system, cracking a nearby window while cooking provides immediate passive relief."
  }
];
---

<Layout title={pageTitle} description={pageDescription} faqs={faqs} availableLocales={['en', 'es', 'pt', 'zh-cn', 'ja']}>
  <!-- Breadcrumbs -->
  <div class="bg-surface-soft border-b border-hairline py-1">
    <div class="max-w-[1280px] mx-auto px-6">
      <Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbs} className="mb-0" />
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="max-w-[1280px] mx-auto px-6 py-10 md:py-16">
    <!-- Header -->
    <header class="max-w-4xl mb-10">
      <div class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wider bg-blue-100 text-blue-900 mb-3">
        <span>💨</span>
        Fluid Mechanics &amp; Environmental Health
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-5xl font-extrabold text-ink tracking-tight mb-4 leading-tight">
        Kitchen Exhaust Range Hood VOC, NO2 &amp; PM2.5 Extraction Efficiency Modeler
      </h1>
      <p class="text-base md:text-xl text-body leading-relaxed">
        Simulate gas stove nitrogen dioxide plumes, cooking oil PM2.5 aerosol dispersal, duct aerodynamic friction, capture velocity, and pediatric asthma inhalation risks.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Interactive Simulator Component -->
    <RangeHoodExtractionModeler lang="en" />

    <!-- Scientific Engineering & Physics Guide -->
    <article class="prose max-w-none border-t border-hairline pt-12 space-y-10 text-body">
      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-4">
          1. The Aerodynamics of Thermal Cooking Plumes
        </h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed">
          When cooking on a residential stovetop, heat from burners and heated cookware creates an upward buoyant convective jet known as the <strong>thermal cooking plume</strong>. As this plume ascends, it entrains surrounding ambient room air, causing it to cool, slow down, and expand outward at a <strong>half-angle of approximately 10° to 15°</strong> (increasing up to 25°–30° during violent high-heat wok searing or shallow frying).
        </p>
        <p class="leading-relaxed mt-3">
          To successfully remove these emissions from the home, a kitchen exhaust hood must satisfy two distinct aerodynamic criteria:
        </p>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6 my-6">
          <div class="p-5 bg-surface-soft rounded-2xl border border-hairline">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mb-2">1. Geometric Containment (Capture)</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
              The range hood must physically overhang the expanding plume footprint. Because the plume widens as it rises, a hood mounted 30 inches above the cooktop receives a plume roughly 6 inches wider than the pan below. A hood that lacks adequate depth (such as a 15-inch deep microwave hood over a 24-inch deep cooktop) allows the outer convective envelope to spill directly into the kitchen breathing zone.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="p-5 bg-surface-soft rounded-2xl border border-hairline">
            <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mb-2">2. Reservoir &amp; Evacuation Velocity (Containment)</h3>
            <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed">
              When food is flipped or oil splatters, the thermal plume surges violently. A range hood requires a <strong>recessed capture sump</strong> (a reservoir 2 to 4 inches deep) to temporarily hold this volumetric surge until the exhaust blower draws it through the grease filters. Flat-bottom hoods lacking a sump bounce the thermal surge sideways into the room.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-4">
          2. Gas Stove Combustion Chemistry: Nitrogen Dioxide (NO2) &amp; Benzene
        </h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed">
          Natural gas ranges burn primarily methane (CH4). In a theoretical environment with 100% pure stoichiometric combustion, methane combines with oxygen to produce solely carbon dioxide (CO2) and water vapor (H2O):
        </p>
        <div class="p-4 bg-surface-soft rounded-xl border border-hairline font-mono text-xs my-4 text-ink overflow-x-auto">
          CH4 + 2 O2 &rarr; CO2 + 2 H2O + Heat
        </div>
        <p class="leading-relaxed">
          However, domestic atmospheric open-flame gas burners operate in ambient air (which is 78% nitrogen). The extreme flame temperature (>2,500°F / 1,400°C) thermally breaks molecular nitrogen bonds via the <em>Zeldovich thermal NOx mechanism</em>, creating substantial quantities of <strong>Nitric Oxide (NO)</strong>, which rapidly oxidizes in ambient air into toxic <strong>Nitrogen Dioxide (NO2)</strong>:
        </p>
        <div class="p-4 bg-surface-soft rounded-xl border border-hairline font-mono text-xs my-4 text-ink overflow-x-auto">
          N2 + O2 &rarr; 2 NO<br/>
          2 NO + O2 &rarr; 2 NO2 (Nitrogen Dioxide)
        </div>
        <p class="leading-relaxed mt-3">
          In addition, landmark research from Stanford University (Kashtan et al. 2023, published in <em>Environmental Science &amp; Technology</em>) established that domestic gas burners emit significant concentrations of unburned <strong>benzene (C6H6)</strong>—a Class 1 human carcinogen linked to leukemia. In unventilated kitchens, indoor benzene levels from gas burners frequently surpass outdoor tobacco smoke concentrations and exceed chronic health reference standards for hours after cooking ceases.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-4">
          3. The Ductless Range Hood Illusion: Why Charcoal Filters Fail on Gas Stoves
        </h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed">
          Many modern multi-family condominiums and renovated homes are equipped with "ductless" or "recirculating" range hoods. These units do not penetrate the exterior building envelope; instead, they pull air through a metal grease mesh and a thin activated charcoal (carbon) filter, exhausting the air back into the kitchen.
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-sm mt-3">
          <li>
            <strong>Zero NO2 / CO Removal:</strong> Activated charcoal operates via physical van der Waals adsorption. It binds high-molecular-weight organic compounds (frying odors, heavy VOCs). However, low-molecular-weight polar inorganic gases like NO2 and CO have negligible adsorption isotherms on standard charcoal at room temperature. <strong>Ductless hoods capture 0% of combustion gases</strong>, recirculating 100% of the toxic NO2 back into the living area.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Rapid Carbon Saturation:</strong> Even for organic VOCs and cooking odors, residential carbon filters contain only a few ounces of active carbon. Once saturated (typically within 60 to 90 days of normal cooking), the filter stops adsorbing entirely, blowing trapped rancid cooking odors back into the room.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Severe Airflow Choke:</strong> Forcing air through a dense carbon bed imposes significant static pressure drop (0.35 to 0.50 in. w.g.), slashing blower airflow by 30% to 45% and producing loud motor strain noise without exterior venting.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-4">
          4. Pediatric Asthma &amp; Respiratory Clinical Epidemiology
        </h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed">
          Children are particularly susceptible to kitchen combustion pollutants because of their higher minute ventilation rate relative to body weight, narrower airway calibers, and developing pulmonary immune systems.
        </p>
        <div class="p-6 bg-surface-soft rounded-2xl border border-hairline my-6">
          <h3 class="text-base font-bold text-ink mb-2">The RMI &amp; Harvard Medical School Findings (Gruenwald et al. 2023)</h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-body leading-relaxed mb-3">
            A comprehensive population attributable fraction (PAF) analysis determined that <strong>12.7% of all childhood asthma in the United States</strong> is directly attributable to indoor gas stove exposure. In states with high gas stove prevalence (such as California and Illinois), this fraction rises to over 20%.
          </p>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 text-center">
            <div class="p-3 bg-white rounded-xl border border-hairline">
              <span class="text-2xl font-black text-red-700 font-mono block">12.7%</span>
              <span class="text-[11px] text-muted">US Child Asthma Attributable to Gas</span>
            </div>
            <div class="p-3 bg-white rounded-xl border border-hairline">
              <span class="text-2xl font-black text-amber-700 font-mono block">+42%</span>
              <span class="text-[11px] text-muted">Increased Asthma Risk (Unventilated)</span>
            </div>
            <div class="p-3 bg-white rounded-xl border border-hairline">
              <span class="text-2xl font-black text-blue-700 font-mono block">106 ppb</span>
              <span class="text-[11px] text-muted">WHO 1-Hour Peak NO2 Limit</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-ink mb-4">
          5. Engineering Sizing: Sump Depth, Duct Aerodynamics &amp; Make-Up Air
        </h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed">
          To guarantee clean indoor air quality, kitchen mechanical systems should be engineered according to established HVAC fluid dynamics:
        </p>
        <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3 text-sm mt-3">
          <li>
            <strong>Sizing Rule of Thumb:</strong> Install a minimum of 100 CFM for every 10,000 BTU of gas cooktop rating (e.g., a 60,000 BTU cooktop mandates at least 600 CFM). For electric and induction cooktops, provide 10–12 CFM per linear inch of width.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Eliminate Accordion Flex Ducts:</strong> Always specify rigid galvanized steel ducting (minimum 7-inch or 8-inch diameter for blowers >400 CFM). Flexible ribbed accordion ducts generate turbulent vortex shedding that chokes blower throughput by 40% to 50% and collects flammable grease in its ridges.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Make-Up Air Depressurization:</strong> High-powered exhaust fans (>400 CFM) can depressurize an airtight modern home, pulling carbon monoxide backward through water heater and furnace flues (back-drafting). Always integrate an automated motorized make-up air damper or crack an adjacent window during high-heat cooking.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Turn the Hood On Before Striking the Flame:</strong> Turning on the exhaust hood 1 to 2 minutes prior to cooking establishes an upward draft pattern in the ductwork, ensuring zero initial plume spillage when high-heat cooking commences.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>
